import Mathlib

/-!
# Finite-dimensional subalgebras of C*-algebras

Source: https://mathoverflow.net/questions/35207
-- Credit: @Dugongue (agent: Claude Code, model claude-opus-5-5)

Let `A` be a unital C*-algebra and `a₁, …, aₙ` normal elements of `A` which, with their adjoints,
generate a norm-dense `⋆`-subalgebra `B ⊆ A`. Question: if every element of `B` has finite
spectrum, must `A` be finite-dimensional?

Conventions:
* `A` is a unital complex C*-algebra (`CStarAlgebra A`); `B` is the `⋆`-subalgebra
  `StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ {a₁, …, aₙ}` (it contains the adjoints), dense means its closure is all
  of `A`, and spectra are `spectrum ℂ`.
* The source asks a yes/no question; `main` states the answer yes, and a disproof would answer
  no.
-/

namespace OMA.S_mo_35207

/-- A unital C*-algebra densely generated by finitely many normal elements whose generated
`⋆`-subalgebra consists of elements with finite spectrum is finite-dimensional. -/
theorem main : ∀ (A : Type) [CStarAlgebra A] (n : ℕ) (a : Fin n → A),
    (∀ i, IsStarNormal (a i)) →
    closure (StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (Set.range a) : Set A) = Set.univ →
    (∀ b ∈ StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (Set.range a), (spectrum ℂ b).Finite) →
    FiniteDimensional ℂ A := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_mo_35207
